\f1\b \cf6 /btw 
\f0\b0 \cf7 The social scrapes (StockTwits/WSB/Nitter) need tight timeout caps + non-blocking so they never gate a heavy scan again \'97 that's what makes the 05:15 heavy run finish in ~10\'9615 min with margin before 6:30. how do we fix it - i cant thouw it away\cf4 \
    \
    You don't throw the social data away \'97 you make it 
\f1\b \cf8 non-blocking
\f0\b0 \cf4  so it never gates the scan. Here's the approach:\
    \
    
\f1\b \cf8 1. Tight per-request timeouts (the immediate bleed)
\f0\b0 \cf4  \
    \
    Right now each StockTwits/WSB/Nitter fetch hangs on its default socket timeout (often 20\'9660s) and retries 3\'d7. For 500 deep-tier tickers that's catastrophic. Cap them hard:\
    - 
\f1\b \cf8 Connect timeout ~3s, read timeout ~5s, 1 retry max
\f0\b0 \cf4  per social source.\
    - A scrape that doesn't answer in ~8s returns empty and moves on. You lose 
\f3\i that ticker's
\f0\i0  social read, not the whole scan.\
    \
    
\f1\b \cf8 2. Decouple social from the bundle write (the real fix)
\f0\b0 \cf4 \
    \
    The scan blocks because social enrichment runs 
\f1\b \cf8 inline, synchronously, before scoring/bundle
\f0\b0 \cf4 . Instead:\
    - 
\f1\b \cf8 Write the bundle on the core data
\f0\b0 \cf4  (price/technicals/fundamentals/options) \'97 the stuff that actually drives the score.\
    - Run social scrapes in a 
\f1\b \cf8 separate background pass
\f0\b0 \cf4  that writes to its own cache (\cf9 cache/social_sentiment.json\cf4 ), and the dashboard/next scan 
\f1\b \cf8 reads it when ready
\f0\b0 \cf4 .\
    - Social sentiment is a 
\f3\i minor
\f0\i0  score pillar and daily-stable \'97 it doesn't need to be fresh-to-the-second in the morning bundle.\
    \
    So the bundle lands in ~10\'9615 min on core data; social fills in async and is fully present by the time you look.\
    \
    
\f1\b \cf8 3. Cap concurrency + circuit-break per source
\f0\b0 \cf4  \
    \
    - Give social its own small worker pool (e.g. 4\'966), and a 
\f1\b \cf8 per-source circuit breaker
\f0\b0 \cf4  (you already have one for yfinance) \'97 after N consecutive failures, skip that source for the rest of the run instead of retrying every ticker.\
    - Nitter especially is fragile; one dead instance shouldn't cost 500 \'d7 timeout.\
    \
    
\f1\b \cf8 4. Tier it (cheapest win)
\f0\b0 \cf4 \
    \
    Social scraping doesn't need all 500 deep-tier names \'97 only the 
\f1\b \cf8 actionable BUY candidates
\f0\b0 \cf4  (top ~50\'96100) have social signal worth reading. Scrape social for the 
\f1\b \cf8 top tier only
\f0\b0 \cf4 , skip the tail. That alone cuts the social workload ~80%.\
  \
    
\f1\b \cf8 Net
\f0\b0 \cf4  \
\
    You keep every bit of social data \'97 it just moves 
\f1\b \cf8 off the critical path
\f0\b0 \cf4 : hard timeouts so no single scrape hangs, an async/background write so the bundle doesn't wait on it, a circuit breaker so a dead source self-skips, and top-tier-only scraping so the volume is small. That's what gets the 05:15 heavy scan finishing in ~10\'9615 min with margin before 6:30, with social still fully populated.\
